hping3 TCP option / timestamp firewall probe tests
===================================================

These tests exercise the --tcp-option custom option builder and the
--tsecr-check timestamp echo verification without needing root, real
firewall hardware, nftables, tcpdump or scapy.

Requirements
------------
  * gcc and GNU make
  * Python 3 (standard library only)
  * unprivileged user namespaces (unshare -Urn), veth and bridge support

Run
---
  make
  TMPDIR=<writable dir> ./tests/run_tests.sh

Layout
------
  unit/test_tcp_options.c
      C unit tests for the option builder and TSecr history ring.

  fwbox/pkt.py
      Minimal IPv4/TCP parse + build helpers and TCP option walking.

  fwbox/firewall.py
      AF_PACKET middlebox emulator with three policies:
        passthrough - RFC 1323 host, echoes the received TSval
        rewrite     - overwrites TSecr with a fixed bogus value
        strip       - removes the timestamp option from its reply

  fwbox/sniff.py
      Captures hping's outgoing packets and prints their option layout,
      used by the wire-format test.

  scenario.sh
      Runs inside an unprivileged namespace; builds a two-namespace veth
      topology and executes one probe scenario.

Topology
--------
An outer user+network namespace runs hping3 on veth-hp. A second network
namespace (created with nested unshare) hosts the Python middlebox on
veth-fw. The middlebox answers from the unconfigured VIP 10.20.0.3 so the
child kernel cannot race it with its own TCP RST; a static neighbour entry
resolves the VIP. AF_PACKET sockets use ETH_P_ALL (0x0003) because binding
a specific ethertype inside an unprivileged user namespace delivers no
frames on some kernels.

What the three firewall probes assert
-------------------------------------
  1. passthrough -> hping prints "TCP timestamp echo: ... OK"
  2. rewrite     -> hping prints "... MISMATCH" with the bogus TSecr
  3. strip       -> hping prints "... option STRIPPED"
